# Performance (Universal)

## Measure before optimizing

- Fix correctness and clarity first. Optimize only bottlenecks backed by profiling, metrics, or known hot paths.
- Do not sacrifice readability for micro-gains unless measured.

## Database & I/O

- Avoid N+1 queries — batch fetch, join, or use eager loading.
- Paginate large lists; never load unbounded result sets into memory.
- Index columns used in WHERE, JOIN, and ORDER BY. Avoid full table scans on large tables.
- Use transactions for related writes; keep transactions short.
- Cache expensive reads with explicit invalidation — not stale-forever caches.

## Application

- Do work asynchronously for slow/non-critical tasks (queues, background jobs).
- Lazy-load heavy modules and route-level code splits in frontends.
- Debounce/throttle high-frequency events (search, scroll, resize).
- Prefer streaming for large files/responses over loading entirely into memory.

## Frontend

- Minimize re-renders: stable keys, memoization where profiling shows benefit.
- Optimize images/assets (size, format, lazy load). Avoid layout thrashing.
- Avoid blocking the main thread with heavy sync work — use workers when needed.

## Resources

- Close connections, file handles, and streams. Use pooling for DB/HTTP clients.
- Set timeouts on external calls. Fail fast rather than hang indefinitely.
- Bound retries with backoff; use circuit breakers for unreliable dependencies.
